<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1820 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Himno de Riego</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A marching song of Spanish liberals from 1820, named after Colonel Rafael del Riego, that twice served as Spain's anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Evaristo Fernández de San Miguel</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>José Melchor Gomis (traditional attribution)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Spanish liberals of the Trienio Liberal; later Spanish republicans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Algeciras, Spain</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written between 31 January and 6 February 1820</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Evaristo Fernández de San Miguel (1820))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>In 1820 Colonel Rafael del Riego led an uprising that restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s liberal constitution of 1812 and opened the period known as the Trienio Liberal (1820–1823). Between 31 January and 6 February 1820, in the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, Evaristo Fernández de San Miguel, a lieutenant colonel of the flying column under Riego's command, wrote the words of a song in his honour; Riego had earlier turned down another text by Antonio Alcalá Galiano. The music is usually attributed to José Melchor Gomis.</p><p>On 7 April 1822 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> Generales approved a decree declaring the song the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. King Ferdinand VII signed it at Aranjuez two days later, and it was published in the Gaceta de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 14 April. In October 1823 Ferdinand overthrew the liberal government and repealed the constitution, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a> became the national anthem again.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1868 overthrew Isabella II, the song had only a modest place in the First Spanish Republic (1873–1874), when &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>&quot; and the &quot;Himno de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>&quot; were more popular. By the time of the Second Spanish Republic (1931–1939) it had regained its popularity and became the chief musical symbol of the government. It was never formally declared the Second Republic's anthem; one reading is that the republicans regarded the 1822 decree as still in force. The republic used a shorter version made from three of the original verses, and the Republican government in exile went on using the song until it dissolved in 1977, after the end of Franco's rule.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> a rude satirical text sung to the tune, mocking the church and the monarchy, was very popular, especially in Catalonia, and opponents of Franco kept singing it after the war.</p><p>Because it was once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s anthem, the song has more than once been played by mistake in place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Che Guevara</a> claimed that when the bell of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was rededicated in 1952, at the Francoist government's expense, after a 1950 earthquake, it played the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a> instead of the Spanish anthem, to the dismay of Spanish officials. It was played in error at a European championship qualifying match in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 1 October 1967, and at the 2003 Davis Cup final in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, where the musician James Morrison performed it; Australian officials blamed an error on the CD supplied to him, and the Spanish sports authorities lodged an official protest.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic marching song of the liberal soldiers of 1820, celebrating their rising for the constitution and urging the defence of the country's freedom. Its mood is martial and confident.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Serenos, alegres,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Calm and cheerful,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">valientes y osados,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>brave and bold,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">cantemos, soldados,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us sing, soldiers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">el himno a la lid.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the hymn to battle.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De nuestros acentos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>At our voices</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">el orbe se admire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let the world wonder</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">y en nosotros mire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and see in us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">los hijos del Cid</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sons of the Cid.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Soldados la patria,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Soldiers, the fatherland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">nos llama a la lid.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>calls us to battle.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Juremos por ella,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us swear by her</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">vencer o morir</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to conquer or to die.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Blandamos el hierro</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us wield the steel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que el tímido esclavo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for the timid slave</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">del fuerte, del bravo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on the strong and brave man’s</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">la faz no osa a ver;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>face dares not look;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">sus huestes cual humo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>his hosts like smoke</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">veréis disipadas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you will see scattered,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">y a nuestras espadas</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and before our swords</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">fugaces correr.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>run swiftly away.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El mundo vio nunca</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The world never saw</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">más noble osadía</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a nobler daring,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ni vio nunca un día</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>nor ever saw a day</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">más grande en valor,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>greater in courage</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que aquel que inflamados</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>than that on which, set alight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">nos vimos del fuego</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we found ourselves on fire</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">que excitara en Riego</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with what had stirred in Riego:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">de Patria el amor</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the love of our country.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Evaristo San Miguel (1820); as quoted in Spanish Wikipedia's article. Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Himno_de_Riego" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Himno de Riego</a>); public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>The first stanza, the refrain and the second and third stanzas of San Miguel’s words.</li><li>Line 1.8: the Cid, Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar (d. 1099), the hero of medieval Castile.</li><li>Lines 4.5–4.8 are one clause in Spanish (“inflamed with the fire that love of country kindled in Riego”); the translation keeps the order of the lines.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
